{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579515130,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU1NTIxOTk1NjI=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4055","origin":"github"},"title":"op.realpath() -\u003e Path.resolve()","message":"As witnessed in a bunch of contexts now (e.g. #4054 #4026) we are susceptible to failure due to windows short paths (these beauties: `C:\\SHORT~1`). We use lexical operations and comparisons on paths in many places, and we always assume full/long paths. Hence this is a big deal on windows. IN particular the GitHub CI machines are set up in a way that make them appear frequently.\n\nAs seen here https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/4054#issuecomment-576150443 switching from `op.realpath` to `Path.resolve()` takes care of this issue in a convenient fashion. Therefore I propose to replace any use of `op.realpath` with the pathlib equivalent. This will not make any such issue go away, but it should substantially reduce our attack surface.","files":null}]}